#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:5
#: 440726843281402b951f552428e10d17
msgid "Tuning QuickStart"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:7
#: c17f3667381d42a1890ef00847e921da
msgid "This article provides information to help you get started with airframe tuning. It includes an overview of the main steps, tools and concepts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:11
#: f44908dc50fd4bbbabe57036d7441868
msgid "How to tune the airframe"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:13
#: 2c7cca2546bc4b709bc68a30e48228c7
msgid "With the default PID settings, Plane will fly the majority of lightweight RC airframes (not full size, full speed airframes) safely, right out of the box. To fly well, with tight navigation and reliable performance in wind, you'll want to tune your autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:18
#: 7f262f25985547308c3e07ccd340c3bb
msgid "The most important configuration is Roll and Pitch tuning, as this is essential for responsive and stable flight as well as effective navigation. The best way to tune the roll and pitch is to use :ref:`Automatic Tuning with AUTOTUNE <automatic-tuning-with-autotune>` (a flight mode that uses changes in flight attitude input by the pilot to learn the key values needed)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:26
#: 40cdc1f10f1a45e4a58dc878ed789ddb
msgid "To use AUTOTUNE you must to be able to fly the aircraft. Plane will fly the majority of lightweight RC airframes right out of the box. We also provide :ref:`configuration values for many common aircraft <configuration-files-for-common-airframes>` that you can use to get your aircraft flying before doing further tuning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:31
#: 1886f3da99fc45f995a96dff23c411d3
msgid "If AUTOTUNE doesn't work with your plane, a fully manual approach is described in the :ref:`Manual Roll, Pitch and Yaw Controller Tuning Guide <new-roll-and-pitch-tuning>` or :ref:`here for firmware versions before 4.1 <roll-pitch-controller-tuning>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:34
#: 7de248306639436bbd54162c4792cb9c
msgid "After tuning the Roll, Pitch (and optionally yaw) you should tune the height controller using the :ref:`TECS tuning guide <tecs-total-energy-control-system-for-speed-height-tuning-guide>` and the horizontal navigation using the \\ :ref:`L1 controller tuning guide <navigation-tuning>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:38
#: 1d91a4286152450d9dccd3417c87d412
msgid "Information on how to tune other aspects of Plane are linked from the :ref:`Tuning landing page. <common-tuning>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:42
#: e391c5ed568841a19c9c3e9d8f1bad6a
msgid "Mission Planner configuration screens"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:44
#: 3567ce1240344870ba60662bcb57a30b
msgid "this section applies only to firmware versions prior to 4.1. This section will be updated in the future for 4.1 and later releases. You can configure the new parameters (See :ref:`Manual Roll, Pitch and Yaw Controller Tuning Guide <new-roll-and-pitch-tuning>`) directly in the Mission Planner CONFIG->Full Parameter List tab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:46
#: 4a25c75467c9412fa625490b6a813604
msgid "The *Mission Planner* provides access to all configuration and tuning parameters through the CONFIG/TUNING section. The column on the left links to the full parameter set. The screen on the right lists some (but not all) of the more commonly modified parameters. You can use either side to make the same parameter change. Parameter updates are sent to the connected board as soon as you press \"write params\", many affect the current flight (if any) immediately, and are written to permanent storage so they survive power cycling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:58
#: 9b42e45d0375469c95392108be129f69
msgid "Mission Planner: CONFIG/TUNINGPlane Pids Screen"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:60
#: 705d19962ed3416faef998c477936e14
msgid "The starting point for Plane tuning is via the Plane PIDs screen shown above. This is where you can tune the Servo Roll and Servo Pitch PID gains to enable flight in FBWA mode, and also enable tuning of the L1 Control for more accurate horizontal positioning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:65
#: bc860284c08b40188096b2d495adf907
msgid "You can also load and save configuration files (to your PC) from the *Advanced Parameters* screen. Configuration files are versioned - an older parameter file found by Plane is ignored if the parameter version number is not current."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:70
#: d967b21443424097ad36bf0585cd8533
msgid "You will note that several of these parameters are PID (Proportional, Integral, Differential) settings which are explained in the next section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:75
#: bb939c0987f642dbb8b259c058a11cdb
msgid "PID gain values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:77
#: 811a41c196cc403a8b65f156815162b2
msgid "The control of roll or pitch angle is adjusted using a `Proportional-Integral-Derivative (PID) Controller <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PID_controller>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:80
#: dee634f1f70c4c97877ed33a30e2d113
msgid "The final control applied to the plane's control surface, is a combination of the effects of four gain values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:83
#: 0e8affa0bdb74e17acff17eda7684ba8
msgid "*Proportional gain (P)* is the simplest form of control, it is the \"present\" error. Autopilot wants 10 degrees of pitch, has 5 degrees, that is an error of 5: apply some amount of elevator (the amount applied for the amount of error is determined - scaled - by the P number)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:88
#: eaca31eed36340e7ad154a99f0a5cfa1
msgid "*Integral gain (I)* takes into consideration previous errors and is able to compensate for steady errors. It can be thought of as an automatic trim adjustment. The disadvantage with the \"I\" gain is that because it is always reacting to past errors, it reduces damping of the control loop as it is always playing 'catchup'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:93
#: 997b0dbf3ebf43d4b207035a4004f150
msgid "*Derivative gain (D)* adds damping because it feeds back the rate of change of the angle. It can also be thought of as attempting to anticipate future changes in angle. The disadvantage of the \"D\" gain is that it increases the amount of noise driving the servo and if turned up too high will cause rapid pitch or roll oscillation that can in some cases damage the aircraft."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:99
#: 8da4bc5f563b406b9bab77f85d0b118f
msgid "*FeedForward gain (FF)* is perhaps the most important since it directly drives the control surfaces from the demanded rate input from the autopilot, much as the pilot does in manual mode. The P,I, and D rate error-based contributions add to this to correct any trim,CG, or external disturbance impacts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:104
#: 4bf8ad0ed1ad4ddcb3ce7a9d1b0f629a
msgid "Tuning FF, P, PI or PID values can improve how quickly an observed error between desired attitude (pitch, speed, bearing, whatever) and actual attitude can be canceled out, without undue oscillation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:110
#: 8d6687582d0a4f2893c36dc8812819ea
msgid "A simple configuration can just specify a P term (I and D is zero and ``INT_MAX`` is not used when I is zero) in firmware versions before 4.1. And FF/P term with I=FF for firmware versions 4.1 and later, with D=0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-quickstart.rst:113
#: 2a9bd73a242d41829f905cac07651f6a
msgid "Refer to :ref:`Manual Roll, Pitch and Yaw Controller Tuning Guide <new-roll-and-pitch-tuning>` or :ref:`here for firmware versions before 4.1 <roll-pitch-controller-tuning>`. for more information"
msgstr ""
